  "live_records": {
    "q-262939": {
      "dom_id": "step-q262939",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If ${log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{k},$ where $k$ is an integer, then $k=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=125$ to base $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{125}⁡x & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡125} & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{{log}_{5}⁡({5}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{log}_{125}⁡x=\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, $k=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
